==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*deuzą}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*dʰewsóm}}.

Germanic cognates include Old Saxon {{term|dior|lang=osx}}, Dutch {{term|dier|lang=nl}}, Old High German {{term|tior|lang=goh}} (German {{term|Tier|lang=de}}), Old Norse {{term|dýr|lang=non}} (Danish {{term|dyr|lang=da}}, Swedish {{term|djur|lang=sv}}), Gothic {{term|𐌳𐌹𐌿𐍃|tr=dius|lang=got}}.  The Indo-European root also led to Lithuanian {{term|dvēsti|lang=lt}}, Latvian {{term|dvēsele|lang=lv}}, Proto-Slavic {{term/t|sla-pro|*duša}} (Russian {{term|душа|tr=duša|lang=ru}}).

